([CASE STUDY 2: 5 Whys Root-Cause Analysis on Hospital Software Errors]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Context: Nurses in an ICU ward committed critical dosage input errors on digital) Tj
T*
(infusion pumps at a rate of 3.2% per 1000 shifts.) Tj
T*
(5 Whys Interrogation Protocol:) Tj
T*
(1. Why did the nurse administer an incorrect medication dosage? -> Because she entered) Tj
T*
(10.0 mg instead of 1.0 mg into the software interface.) Tj
T*
(2. Why did she enter 10.0 instead of 1.0? -> Because the touchscreen decimal point) Tj
T*
(button was tiny and failed to register on the first tap, and she double-tapped.) Tj
T*
(3. Why did she fail to notice the double-tap zero? -> Because the screen display lacked) Tj
T*
(prominent confirmation contrast and alarm warnings for out-of-range clinical dosages.) Tj
T*
(4. Why was she in a rush and distracted? -> Because the software system sounded 120) Tj
T*
(false positive operational alarms per hour, causing severe cognitive alarm fatigue.) Tj
T*
(5. Why are there so many false alarms? -> Because the software engineers set universal) Tj
T*
(sensitivity thresholds across all telemetry variables without consulting ICU nurses on) Tj
T*
(clinical tolerances.) Tj
T*
(Root-Cause Solution: Redesigned the alert taxonomy with smart clinical tolerance ranges,) Tj
T*
(reducing false alarms by 78%, and implemented mandatory dual-nurse slider confirmation) Tj
T*
(for high-risk dosages.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
